WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.960
Picture the electron transport chain and ATP synthase as a couple in a committed

2
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.840
relationship. Normally they are tightly coupled.

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.000
The chain builds the proton gradient, and ATP synthase spends it to make ATP.

4
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.200
Uncoupling is the breakup.

5
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.640
Protons find another way back across the membrane instead of going through ATP

6
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.240
synthase, so the energy of the gradient is released as heat rather than captured as

7
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.660
ATP. The chain keeps pumping furiously, oxygen consumption climbs, but the

8
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.140
partnership no longer produces anything useful.

9
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:39.013
This table forces you to reason like the chain itself.

10
00:00:39.653 --> 00:00:44.173
An ETC inhibitor, such as rotenone or cyanide, shuts everything down.

11
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:49.373
Oxygen consumption, ATP synthesis and the proton gradient all fall.

12
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:55.793
An ATP synthase inhibitor, oligomycin, blocks the exit for protons, so synthesis

13
00:00:55.793 --> 00:00:59.613
falls and consumption falls, but the gradient backs up and rises.

14
00:01:00.313 --> 00:01:02.553
The uncoupler is the counterintuitive case.

15
00:01:02.553 --> 00:01:08.293
ATP synthesis falls and the gradient collapses, yet oxygen consumption increases.

16
00:01:08.993 --> 00:01:13.733
Why? Because with the gradient leaking away, the chain races to rebuild it, pumping

17
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:15.493
faster and burning more oxygen.

18
00:01:16.253 --> 00:01:20.533
If you can predict each row, you understand how coupling actually works.

19
00:01:21.383 --> 00:01:26.483
Electrons refuse to flow through the transport chain unless ADP is available to be

20
00:01:26.483 --> 00:01:33.063
turned into ATP. Watch the oxygen consumption trace, respiration idles until ADP is

21
00:01:33.063 --> 00:01:38.323
added, then the rate jumps, and it falls again once the supply of ADP is nearly

22
00:01:38.323 --> 00:01:44.323
exhausted. This is acceptor control, also called respiratory control, and it is the

23
00:01:44.323 --> 00:01:46.903
cell's way of matching fuel burning to demand.

24
00:01:46.903 --> 00:01:49.403
ADP is the signal for need.

25
00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:54.723
When the cell has spent its ATP, the ADP it generates lifts the brake on the chain.

26
00:01:55.263 --> 00:02:00.203
This is a clean example of metabolism being governed by energy charge rather than by

27
00:02:00.203 --> 00:02:01.203
substrate alone.

28
00:02:01.967 --> 00:02:03.867
ATP synthase is reversible.

29
00:02:04.447 --> 00:02:08.867
During normal respiration, the proton gradient drives it forward to make ATP.

30
00:02:09.507 --> 00:02:15.187
But in ischemia or severe hypoxia, oxygen runs low, the chain stalls, and the

31
00:02:15.187 --> 00:02:16.187
gradient collapses.

32
00:02:16.927 --> 00:02:21.987
Now the enzyme can spin backward, hydrolyzing the cell's remaining ATP in a futile

33
00:02:21.987 --> 00:02:26.587
attempt to rebuild the gradient, wasting precious energy exactly when it is

34
00:02:26.587 --> 00:02:31.147
scarcest. The inhibitory factor IF1 is the emergency brake.

35
00:02:31.147 --> 00:02:36.767
It binds at low pH, which marks oxygen deprivation, and blocks the reverse rotation,

36
00:02:37.187 --> 00:02:38.607
protecting cellular ATP.
